WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.402
Request validation is the first defensive
boundary in many Go REST APIs.

2
00:00:03.482 --> 00:00:05.575
Structs describe what code expects after
decoding,

3
00:00:05.655 --> 00:00:08.700
but they do not prove that the request is
complete, meaningful, authorized,

4
00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:09.684
or within safe limits.

5
00:00:09.764 --> 00:00:12.523
Request bodies, query strings, path
parameters, headers, cookies,

6
00:00:12.603 --> 00:00:15.886
and content types should be treated as
untrusted until the server validates them.

7
00:00:15.966 --> 00:00:16.917
Start before decoding.

8
00:00:16.997 --> 00:00:19.899
A handler should confirm the method and
content type where appropriate,

9
00:00:19.979 --> 00:00:20.931
apply a body size limit,

10
00:00:21.011 --> 00:00:23.532
and decide how malformed or unsupported
input will be handled.

11
00:00:23.612 --> 00:00:25.182
JSON decoding is a boundary operation.

12
00:00:25.262 --> 00:00:28.592
It should not silently turn an
uncontrolled request into trusted application data

13
00:00:28.672 --> 00:00:30.575
simply because the decode call filled a
struct.

14
00:00:30.655 --> 00:00:33.224
Validation should cover the facts the
business logic relies on.

15
00:00:33.304 --> 00:00:36.634
Required fields, unknown fields where
relevant, string lengths, numeric ranges,

16
00:00:36.714 --> 00:00:38.950
enum values, array lengths, timestamps,
identifiers,

17
00:00:39.030 --> 00:00:41.028
and relationships between fields may all
matter.

18
00:00:41.108 --> 00:00:43.820
A value can be syntactically valid JSON
and still be outside policy,

19
00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:46.897
inconsistent with the workflow, or
inappropriate for the current caller.

20
00:00:46.977 --> 00:00:50.117
The boundary layer should convert external
request data into internal types.

21
00:00:50.197 --> 00:00:53.289
Downstream code is simpler when it
receives a value that already passed size,

22
00:00:53.369 --> 00:00:55.130
shape, meaning, and normalization checks.

23
00:00:55.210 --> 00:00:57.874
That pattern also keeps validation tests
focused and reviewable.

24
00:00:57.954 --> 00:00:59.999
Rejection behavior should be safe and
consistent.

25
00:01:00.079 --> 00:01:03.172
Malformed JSON, oversized payloads,
missing fields, unsupported formats,

26
00:01:03.252 --> 00:01:06.725
and invalid combinations should produce
understandable responses without exposing

27
00:01:06.805 --> 00:01:07.566
internal details.

28
00:01:07.646 --> 00:01:09.787
The point of validation is not only to
block bad input.

29
00:01:09.867 --> 00:01:12.341
It preserves invariants and makes later
code easier to trust.

30
00:01:12.421 --> 00:01:14.561
Validation should also be documented
through tests.

31
00:01:14.641 --> 00:01:17.258
A team should know which inputs are
accepted, which are rejected,

32
00:01:17.338 --> 00:01:19.003
and what response shape clients receive.

33
00:01:19.083 --> 00:01:22.508
That turns validation from scattered
defensive code into a contract that can survive

34
00:01:22.588 --> 00:01:25.300
refactoring, framework changes, and client
updates across teams.
